{"name":"Country Cottage","hex":"#d9c1b7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"country","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The territory of a nation; a sovereign state or a region once independent and still distinct in institutions, language, etc. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A large area regarded as distinct from other areas, e.g. because it is inhabited by a specific group of people"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["From or in the countryside, connected with it, or typical of it.","Of or connected to country music."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/country"},{"term":"cottage","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small house.","A seasonal home of any size or stature, a recreational home or a home in a remote location."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To stay at a seasonal home, to go cottaging.","To have homosexual sex in a public lavatory; to practice cottaging."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cottage"}],"entries":[{"title":"Country","blocks":[{"text":"A country is a political entity or its territory in the world. They function as sovereign states, unrecognized states, constituent countries, or dependent territories. A country may consist of multiple nations, a structure known as a multinational state. Countries are often distinguished as developing countries or developed countries. There is no universal agreement on the number of \"countries\" in the world. This ambiguity is a result of several countries not being recognized as sovereign states by the UN system, but are recognized by at least one UN member, known as \"diplomatic recognition\". These countries often have disputed territories and have only partial recognition. A number of entities without recognition are also commonly considered a country."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Official_United_Nations_World_Map_-_October_2022.svg/330px-Official_United_Nations_World_Map_-_October_2022.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Country"},{"title":"Cottage","blocks":[{"text":"A cottage during England's feudal period was the holding by a cottager of a small house with enough garden to feed a family and in return for the cottage, the cottager had to provide some form of service to the manorial lord. However, in time cottage just became the general term for a small house. In modern usage, a cottage is usually a modest, often cosy dwelling, typically in a rural or semi-rural location and not necessarily in England. The cottage orné, often quite large and grand residences built by the nobility, dates back to a movement of \"rustic\" stylised cottages of the late 18th and early 19th century during the Romantic movement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Country_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Country_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cottage"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Oceania_without_Asian_country_codes.jpg/500px-Oceania_without_Asian_country_codes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Oceania_without_Asian_country_codes.jpg/500px-Oceania_without_Asian_country_codes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A number of non-sovereign entities nevertheless have country codes, such as PF (French Polynesia) and TK (Tokelau)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOceania_without_Asian_country_codes.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Handschrift_Brussel_p-37-38.jpg/500px-Handschrift_Brussel_p-37-38.jpg?utm_source=en.wik